            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "unescape_text turns HTML escapes back into the characters they stand for.\n\nText out of a web page, a feed or some network services arrives with characters written as codes: \"&amp;\" for an ampersand, \"&lt;\" and \"&gt;\" for angle brackets, \"&quot;\" for a double quote, \"&eacute;\" for an accented e, \"&#8212;\" or \"&#x2014;\" for a long dash. Until they are turned back, the string looks wrong when shown and fails to match words it should match.\n\nEvery named and numbered escape HTML knows is handled. Anything else is left as it is.\n\nONE TO WATCH:\n\"&nbsp;\" becomes a non-breaking space. It looks like a space but is not one, so splitting on spaces will not split there - and printable, which keeps only plain characters, removes it entirely, joining the words on either side.\n\nWHAT IT TAKES:\nAnything; it is turned into a string first. A list of words is joined with spaces, and line breaks are removed.\n\nSYNTAX:\nunescape_text\n\nINPUTS:\n\nescaped string in:\nThe text. Receiving it triggers the node.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nunescaped string out:\nThe same text with the escapes replaced.\n\nRELATED:\nprintable removes characters rather than translating them. string_replace for any other substitution.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 790,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 560,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "unescape_text turns HTML escapes back into the characters they stand for.\n\nText out of a web page, a feed or some network services arrives with characters written as codes: \"&amp;\" for an ampersand, \"&lt;\" and \"&gt;\" for angle brackets, \"&quot;\" for a double quote, \"&eacute;\" for an accented e, \"&#8212;\" or \"&#x2014;\" for a long dash. Until they are turned back, the string looks wrong when shown and fails to match words it should match.\n\nEvery named and numbered escape HTML knows is handled. Anything else is left as it is.\n\nONE TO WATCH:\n\"&nbsp;\" becomes a non-breaking space. It looks like a space but is not one, so splitting on spaces will not split there - and printable, which keeps only plain characters, removes it entirely, joining the words on either side.\n\nWHAT IT TAKES:\nAnything; it is turned into a string first. A list of words is joined with spaces, and line breaks are removed.\n\nSYNTAX:\nunescape_text\n\nINPUTS:\n\nescaped string in:\nThe text. Receiving it triggers the node.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nunescaped string out:\nThe same text with the escapes replaced.\n\nRELATED:\nprintable removes characters rather than translating them. string_replace for any other substitution."
        },
